  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently over the eastern CONUS with ridging \nover much of the Plains and into the southwest US. An upper \nshortwave trough is sliding across the northwestern US into the \nNorthern Rockies. In our area, scattered showers continue across \nportions of central KS due to 850-700 mb WAA. This late afternoon \ninto this evening, slight chances for showers and storms will be \npossible in eastern Kansas as 850 mb WAA continues. Strong to severe \nstorms appear unlikely with this activity due to the lack of \ninstability. As we move into late tonight and the overnight hours, \nadditional storm chances will be possible for areas along and east \nof I-135 with the best chances in southeast KS as 700 mb WAA \ncontinues. Strong to marginally severe storms will be possible with \nthis activity as MUCAPE values are generally around 1000 J/kg with \n30-40 kts of effective shear and mid-level lapse rates greater than \n7 C/km. The main hazard will likely be large hail but isolated \ndamaging winds cannot be ruled out. PWATs are around 1.7\" which is \ngreater than the 75th percentile for this time of year, leading to \nefficient rainfall with any storm that develops. If numerous storms \ncontinue to track over the same area, localized flooding concerns \nmay be possible. \n\nMoving into Monday morning, scattered showers and storms may linger \nfrom overnight activity in areas along and east of the Flint Hills \nwith this activity expected to shift east by late Monday morning. \nHigh temperatures will rise back into the 90s with the warmest \nreadings anticipated in south-central KS. For Monday night, mostly \ndry conditions are expected before slight rain chances start to \nincrease in northern Kansas, closer to early Tuesday morning as a \nweak shortwave slides across the region.  \n\nFor Tuesday, high temperatures will once again rise into the low to \nmid 90s across our area. As we move towards Tuesday night into \nWednesday, rain chances will increase as a stronger shortwave moves \ninto the Upper Mississippi Valley and Great Lakes area, pushing a \nfrontal boundary south into the Central Plains. Showers and storms \nmay be possible along this weak boundary, though timing and coverage \nare still uncertain with this activity. \n\nFor Wednesday and beyond, the upper air pattern will generally \nremain unchanged, keeping the Plains in northwesterly flow aloft. \nThis will allow embedded shortwaves to continue ejecting out into \nthe region, keeping periodic rain chances possible into next \nweekend. The main rain chances will be in the evenings through the \novernight hours each night. For temperatures, highs will remain in \nthe 90s with values around or slight above normal temperatures for \nthis time of year. Lows will be in the upper 60s to around 70 across \nthe area. For next weekend, triple digit heat is possible to return \nas the upper ridge axis shifts towards the Northern Plains.",
